WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.000
Think the Inquisitor Marrok is the next big&nbsp;
thing in Star Wars? Think again. Turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.340
this franchise has got a real bad guy problem.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:12.300
The Star Wars franchise has developed a&nbsp;
nasty habit of wasting cool characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.260
before they've realized their full potential.&nbsp;
If a villainous character is the brooding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:20.520
silent type, chances are they'll be&nbsp;
disposed of pretty quickly. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:25.860
this trend has continued with Ahsoka, which kills&nbsp;
off the mysterious Inquisitor Marrok in Episode 4.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:30.180
The unfortunate moment occurs when Ahsoka&nbsp;
Tano slices the Inquisitor's midriff during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:34.800
a chaotic lightsaber battle in a forest.&nbsp;
Afterward, Marrok disappears into thin air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.840
presumably to never return again. The green&nbsp;
smoke that accompanies his disappearance also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.220
suggests that Marrok is a zombie who was&nbsp;
created by the Nightsisters of Dathomir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:47.640
which should end the theory that he's&nbsp;
actually Ezra Bridger in disguise.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.240
While the nature of Marrok's death serves&nbsp;
to enhance the lore of the Galaxy, Far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:55.080
Far Away, his exit from Ahsoka&nbsp;
feels a little premature. He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.920
essentially this series' version of the&nbsp;
original Boba Fett and Captain Phasma,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.760
both of whom also suffered underwhelming&nbsp;
stories in the grand scheme of things.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.900
So does Star Wars have an issue when it&nbsp;
comes to creating compelling arcs for its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.800
coolest mysterious characters? Let's look&nbsp;
at the history, starting with the bounty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.640
hunter Boba Fett. His first appearance in&nbsp;
The Empire Strikes Back led us to believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.720
that he was going to be a formidable villain&nbsp;
— but he turned out to be pretty much useless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.280
as evidenced by his anti-climactic fall&nbsp;
into the Sarlacc pit in Return of the Jedi.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.320
"Laugh it up, fuzzball."

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.960
Similarly, the Star Wars sequel trilogy&nbsp;
introduces Captain Phasma as a powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.140
Stormtrooper who seems like she's&nbsp;
going to be a constant thorn in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.940
Finn and Rey's sides. Unfortunately, Phasma is&nbsp;
killed off rather abruptly in The Last Jedi.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:46.320
Of course, The Book of Boba Fett series on&nbsp;
Disney+ and the Phasma novel add more depth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.280
to their respective characters' backstories. But&nbsp;
that doesn't mean that they weren't wasted from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:54.660
the get-go, and it remains to be seen if Marrok&nbsp;
will get his own expanded story down the line.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:59.340
While Ahsoka presents Marrok as a serious&nbsp;
threat, his death in Episode 4 means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.060
the show has missed an opportunity to&nbsp;
dig into his origins. His association&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.080
with Morgan Elsbeth and the Daughters of&nbsp;
Dathomir implies that he's a Nightbrother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:11.220
a society whose history hasn't really been&nbsp;
covered in great detail in the live-action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:15.600
realm of the Star Wars franchise. Everyone loves&nbsp;
a little mystery, of course, but the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.500
Marrok has robbed fans of the perfect vehicle&nbsp;
to explore this side of the Star Wars galaxy.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:24.660
Moreover, Marrok is an Inquisitor, meaning&nbsp;
that he was once part of an elite death squad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.400
and an heir to the Sith. While they aren't&nbsp;
full Sith per se, Inquisitors are perfectly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.480
capable of using elements of the Force to&nbsp;
inflict damage on their opponents. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.780
Ahsoka doesn't give this villain an&nbsp;
opportunity to really showcase his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:41.460
abilities outside of a couple of fight scenes.&nbsp;
Ahsoka eliminates him with relative ease too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:45.180
which just adds to the notion that Marrok&nbsp;
was created to be a throwaway character.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:50.280
Granted, it's possible that Ahsoka will unveil&nbsp;
more Marrok-esque assassins down the line; Morgan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:55.980
Baylan Skoll, and Shin Hati need soldiers to aid&nbsp;
them in their quest to find Grand Admiral Thrawn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.600
after all. That said, Marrok is an inherently&nbsp;
cool character who got fans speculating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.140
and losing him has brought an end&nbsp;
to some interesting discourse.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:09.060
And Marrok's death has certainly spurred some fans&nbsp;
to share their views. One Twitter user writes:

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:12.000
"Alas, poor Marrok. We barely knew ye."

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Another echoed that sentiment,&nbsp;
stating that the character had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.160
lots of potential to grow and develop. They wrote:

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.360
"I think a former Inquisitor turned mercenary&nbsp;
could be a super cool recurring character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.500
in Star Wars lore. Potential for a new&nbsp;
Boba Fett level of mysterious badass."

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:30.180
That said, while many fans were sad to say&nbsp;
goodbye to the Inquisitor, one Redditor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.260
believes that Marrok's death is actually quite&nbsp;
fitting for someone of his ilk. They wrote:

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:38.760
"Can't escape the Inquisitor tradition of&nbsp;
going out like a punk against Ahsoka I guess."

